Title: Judge's Crossword Puzzle No. 66
Author: E. Stebbins
Date: 1928-08-25
Byline: Submitted by E. Stebbins, Austin, Texas.
Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1928-08-25_95/page/n31/mode/1up
Puzzle-Number: 66
Solution-Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1928-09-01_95/page/n31/mode/1up


RADIO#A#WATTS
ALE#DENSE#RAT
GLADES#ARMADA
E#COST#VEIN#I
SLOW#EGO#SCAR
#INNER#RITES#
ON##L###N##PA
#GRAFT#SKATE#
POUR#EVE#DONS
I#GEAR#PEAL#I
PAGANS#ARMLET
PIE#TEALS#ERE
ADDLE#T#TURNS


A1. This brings things home to you. ~ RADIO
A6. What electrical power is measured in. ~ WATTS
A10. It's a crime to drink this in the land of the free. ~ ALE
A11. What we think the population of a prohibition country is. ~ DENSE
A14. A nasty little animal. ~ RAT
A15. Open spaces in a forest. ~ GLADES
A17. What Philip II. of Spain sent to England. ~ ARMADA
A19. The price she paid. ~ COST
A20. A membranous tube. ~ VEIN
A21. There's no speed in this kind of a boat. ~ SLOW
A23. No Broadway star lacks this. ~ EGO
A24. A mark of combat. ~ SCAR
A26. This tube should be taken on a trip. ~ INNER
A28. Ceremonies. ~ RITES
A30. A preposition. (The first letter is o.) ~ ON
A31. The lesser half. ~ PA
A32. This blinds the eyes of Federal agents. ~ GRAFT
A36. A cold-weather slipper. ~ SKATE
A39. To cause liquor to flow. ~ POUR
A40. This was created for Adam's express company. ~ EVE
A41. What one does who puts on. ~ DONS
A43. This has a lot of teeth. ~ GEAR
A45. What the bells do. ~ PEAL
A47. These have heathenish dispositions. ~ PAGANS
A49. A heathenish ornament. ~ ARMLET
A51. This isn't much good when half-baked. ~ PIE
A52. These grow down in water. ~ TEALS
A54. Before. ~ ERE
A55. Muddle. ~ ADDLE
A56. What acrobats do. ~ TURNS

D1. What the irate parent does. ~ RAGES
D2. Every one ~ ALL
D3. A church officer. ~ DEACON
D4. Poetic compositions. ~ ODES
D5. Indefinite article. ~ AN
D6. Part of the verb "to be." ~ WERE
D7. A state—in which there are no prohibition laws. ~ TRANCE
D8. A well-known cartoonist of a few years ago. ~ TAD
D9. Climbers often use this to get to a higher level. ~ STAIR
D12. A feminine name. ~ ESTER
D13. This makes a lot of difference—with a meal. ~ SAVOR
D16. What a little shaver takes off. ~ DOWN
D18. This is vaporous. ~ MIST
D22. What you hear "east side, west side—and all around the town." ~ LINGO
D25. Pertaining to the asp. ~ ASPEN
D27. A little mythological creature. ~ ELF
D29. Be careful what you say with this. ~ INK
D33. This kind of country has its ups and downs. ~ RUGGED
D34. Vacant ground; level space. ~ AREA
D35. This kind of speech is pretty smooth. ~ TERSE
D36. One of the divisions of the calyx, calyx-leaf. ~ SEPAL
D37. This fellow raised Cain long ago. ~ ADAM
D38. One who tolls a bell. ~ TOLLER
D39. A peasant girl in one of Robert Browning's poems. ~ PIPPA
D42. X marks these in the tabloids. ~ SITES
D44. To feed the kitty. ~ ANTE
D46. Formerly, of old. (Archaic.) ~ ERST
D48. This always is helpful. ~ AID
D50. A bird often found in Crossword Puzzles. ~ ERN
D53. A preposition of place. ~ AT


